00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.610 align:start position:0%
 
Prime Minister you're just about to go  to meet some child refugees in a school  do you feel vindicated by an approach  that says that those refugees should  stay here in the region when some have  criticized you back home for not saying  more should come to the United Kingdom  well I think it's very important as the  UK government has done since the  beginning of the Syrian crisis that we  do predominantly provide aid in the  region so we can support people so  they're close to home so when we do see  a peaceful solution to the conflict in  Syria and people are able to return home  they're able to do that but but while  they are refugees in countries like  Jordan which I'll be visiting visitings  and refugees today we actually prove our  helped provide education and training so  they have the skills they need when they  finally return home so the kids who stay  here in the region I think it's  important that we support of course  we're taking refugee children into the  United Kingdom but the view we took was  that it was important to ensure that  those we bring to the United Kingdom  those who are most vulnerable who  particularly require the help and  support that we can give in the UK but  for the majority we can help far more  refugees by the age we're putting into  the region our total aid as a result of  the Syrian crisis it's 2.3 billion  pounds we've been the second biggest  bilateral donor overall that means we  have been providing food water medical  supplies and yes education and training  to young people to refugees to help them  with their futures on Saudi Arabia isn't  it just the reality that you are not  going to raise with King Salman the  issue of jailed dissidents the issue of  the use by the Saudis of cluster bombs  made in Britain against Yemen well you  have no difficulty in raising hard  issues with those that we meet with  whether bearding Saudi Arabia or  elsewhere around the world what matters  is that we have the relationship of  engagement that enables us to do that  but we will also be talking about the  very important relationship the UK has  with Saudi Arabia an important  relationship yes in terms of trade but  also in terms of security our  cooperation the saudis cooperation with  us has helped to save lives in the UK  is there a pattern developing in your  foreign trips people are relating it to  brexit are we downplaying human rights  concerns with your visits to Saudi  Arabia to Turkey where's this strong  British tradition of standing up for  human rights the strong British  tradition of standing up for human  rights is there the stronger Jewish  tradition of standing up for British  national interest is there I want to see  the United Kingdom as a truly global  Britain outward looking around the world  trading around the world yes a good  continuing deep partnership with the  European Union but also trading and  working with others around the whole of  the globe that's what we will be doing  in future that's what I'm working on  yes it's about trade that's about jobs  back home in the United Kingdom but it's  also about our security security for the  UK and us working with others to  maintain security around the world  so Prime Minister you mention that  phrase truly global nation in the future  but we're standing here in Jordan  currently we have a free trade  arrangement with Jordan through the EU I  mean the risk is we lose that so it's  not about being more global how do you  protect what we've got already in terms  of how global we are no it will be  possible for us to continue trade  arrangements with countries where we've  previously had them through the EU once  we're outside the European Union but it  also means that we can negotiate our own  REIT raid arrangements with various  parts of the world I think that's going  to be important to us we want to have a  deep and special partnership continuing  with the European Union but yes we want  to build our relationships with old  friends and new allies alike around the  world in terms of your strategic  objectives before roughly this point  we're now through the gate of article 50  you wanted pre negotiations we didn't  get them you wanted parallel  negotiations on the exit deal and  article 50 they've said no you talked  about No Deal doesn't look like anybody  believes that threat haven't your key  strategic aims not been hit as we  trigger article 50 no you're quite wrong  I mean let's first of all let's look at  the whole question of the where we end  up at the end of this negotiation have  we look will we have looked at both  withdraw and the future relationship  that's what's important that's what I'm  that's what I'm asking for and that's  what I believe increasingly we will we  will see  you will see from the European Union  various people have commented that they  think we should get on to looking at  that future relationship soon and we  will do that what's important is that  when we leave the EU people know what  the future relationship is it's common  sense it's pragmatic for people it's  pragmatic for businesses and I believe  that's what we'll be working for I think  it's what both sides will be working for  on No Deal in your Lancaster House  speech the talk around that was turning  us into a kind of singapore-style Haven  after that Boris Johnson says actually  no deal is perfectly okay and then in  the article 50 letter it there is this  sort of implied security threat to seem  slightly chaotic and so what we've said  is very clear that is that we expect to  get and we want to get the best possible  deal for the United Kingdom  I believe that's possible because it  won't just be a good deal for us it will  be a good deal for the rest of the  European Union on security issue it's a  very practical reason for raising it in  the letter we're currently a member of  certain programs certain systems around  Europe that help us to cooperate on  exchanging information about terrorists  